WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.450
Debug and maintenance features are useful
during development, manufacturing,

2
00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.460
and field support.

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.909
JTAG, SWD, UART consoles, bootloader
commands, factory test modes,

4
00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:12.010
diagnostic shells, trace output,
provisioning interfaces,

5
00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.401
and service commands can save time when
engineers need visibility.

6
00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:18.153
The question is what remains available in
production.

7
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:21.254
Production hardening starts by listing
those capabilities.

8
00:00:21.334 --> 00:00:24.703
Teams should know which interfaces exist,
which builds enable them,

9
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:27.339
what commands they provide, what state
they expose,

10
00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:29.104
and what actions they can perform.

11
00:00:29.184 --> 00:00:32.960
A feature that is harmless on a lab bench
may be inappropriate in a field build.

12
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.119
Factory modes should not accidentally
become field exposure.

13
00:00:36.199 --> 00:00:40.323
A manufacturing command may calibrate
hardware, install identity, clear storage,

14
00:00:40.403 --> 00:00:42.378
run self-tests, or enable special logs.

15
00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:46.583
Those commands should have a documented
production policy so that release builds do

16
00:00:46.663 --> 00:00:49.916
not inherit access paths meant only for
controlled environments.

17
00:00:49.996 --> 00:00:52.029
Bootloader access requires clear rules.

18
00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:56.466
The bootloader may provide update,
recovery, configuration, or diagnostic functions.

19
00:00:56.546 --> 00:01:00.496
Reviewers should understand how access is
reached, which commands are enabled,

20
00:01:00.576 --> 00:01:04.585
what state is required, and how production
settings are verified during release.

21
00:01:04.665 --> 00:01:08.847
Diagnostic commands need authorization
decisions where the product requires them.

22
00:01:08.927 --> 00:01:11.135
Some commands may be safe for normal
support.

23
00:01:11.215 --> 00:01:14.236
Others may expose sensitive state or
change device behavior.

24
00:01:14.316 --> 00:01:17.975
A simple command list with ownership,
build profile, access requirement,

25
00:01:18.055 --> 00:01:20.263
and logging behavior can prevent
confusion.

26
00:01:20.343 --> 00:01:24.119
Build profiles should clearly separate
development, manufacturing, test,

27
00:01:24.199 --> 00:01:25.303
and release behavior.

28
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:29.217
Compiler flags, logging levels,
assertions, debug ports, service commands,

29
00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:32.956
and provisioning paths should be reviewed
as part of the release baseline.

30
00:01:33.036 --> 00:01:36.580
Production hardening is strongest when it
is repeatable and auditable.
